### Why does the JVM not exit after main()?

The JVM does not exit when main() returns. It exits when all non-daemon threads have terminated. Thread pool workers created by `Executors.newFixedThreadPool()` are non-daemon by default. They sit in a loop calling `queue.take()`, blocking forever waiting for tasks that never come.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class JvmHangDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        pool.submit(() -> System.out.println("Trade settled"));

        System.out.println("main() finished!");
        // main() returns but JVM does NOT exit!
        // 10 pool threads are non-daemon, alive, waiting for tasks.
        // Process hangs forever. Must kill with kill -9!
    }
}
```

```text
Trade settled
main() finished!
(process hangs indefinitely)
```

### How do you fix the problem?

Always call `pool.shutdown()` when you are done submitting tasks.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CleanShutdownDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        try {
            pool.submit(() -> System.out.println("Trade settled"));
        } finally {
            pool.shutdown(); // stop accepting, finish queued tasks
            boolean done = pool.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            if (!done) {
                pool.shutdownNow(); // force stop
            }
        }
        System.out.println("JVM exits cleanly");
    }
}
```

```text
Trade settled
JVM exits cleanly
```

### What is the production shutdown pattern?

The standard pattern handles both graceful and forced shutdown scenarios.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ProductionShutdown {
    private static final ExecutorService pool =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    static {
        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Shutdown hook: stopping pool...");
            pool.shutdown();
            try {
                if (!pool.awaitTermination(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                    System.out.println("Forcing shutdown...");
                    pool.shutdownNow();
                    pool.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                pool.shutdownNow();
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
            System.out.println("Pool terminated");
        }));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        pool.submit(() -> System.out.println("Processing payment"));
        // Shutdown hook runs on SIGTERM (kill), SIGINT (Ctrl+C)
    }
}
```

### What are the common non-daemon threads that prevent exit?

Several Java APIs create non-daemon threads by default:

- `ExecutorService` threads (Executors.newFixedThreadPool, etc.)
- `ScheduledExecutorService` threads
- `java.util.Timer` thread
- AWT/Swing Event Dispatch Thread
- RMI and JMX threads
- Any Thread created without `setDaemon(true)`

### Can you use a daemon ThreadFactory instead?

Yes, but with a trade-off. Daemon pool threads let the JVM exit without calling shutdown(), but in-flight tasks may be killed mid-execution.

```java
ExecutorService daemonPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10, r -> {
    Thread t = new Thread(r);
    t.setDaemon(true); // won't prevent JVM exit
    return t;
});
// Good for: optional background tasks (cache warming, metrics)
// Bad for: critical tasks (payments, settlements)
```

### How is shutdown() different from shutdownNow()?

| Feature | shutdown() | shutdownNow() |
|---|---|---|
| New tasks | Rejected | Rejected |
| Running tasks | Finish normally | Interrupted |
| Queued tasks | Execute then stop | Returned as list, not executed |
| Return value | void | List<Runnable> (unexecuted) |

### What about try-with-resources for ExecutorService?

Since Java 19, ExecutorService implements AutoCloseable. The close() method calls shutdown() and awaitTermination() automatically.

```java
// Java 19+
try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10)) {
    pool.submit(() -> System.out.println("Trade settled"));
} // pool.close() called automatically: shutdown + await
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does shutdown() block? No, it returns immediately. Use awaitTermination() to block.
- Can you submit tasks after shutdown()? No, it throws RejectedExecutionException.
- When does the shutdown hook NOT run? On SIGKILL (kill -9), JVM crash, or Runtime.halt().
- How does Docker stop interact with shutdown hooks? Docker sends SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL after a timeout (default 10s).
